Most of the time, when a prototype of a new car is assembled, and everything works perfectly on the first ignition, people will jump up and down and shout, ‘We have all the loops in our wiring harness right!’ It all looks beautiful.
The above two scenes are indeed sometimes more difficult, but can we say that our design is perfect?
In your actual work, you have encountered such similar problems more or less:
An unlikely operation (it is a regular operation, but some unfavorable factors happened to happen) resulted in the burning of a critical component and the abnormal EPS function.
Heavy rain in the south in summer, the vehicle was immersed in water, the oxygen sensor connector was flooded, and the function failed. Is it very familiar?
So, what is a good design?
Right, what I’m saying is, design with as many failure modes in mind as possible. In each of these failure modes, you have the corresponding solution.
Did you take your design into consideration?
If not, isn’t your design running naked?
A systems engineer who does not know failure mode and does not know validation is always a half-harness engineer.
