Mëllerdall, with an area of 256 square kilometers and a population of around 25.500, is located in the center of the Trier-Luxembourg basin. Welcome the Luxembourg Sandstone formation, one of the most spectacular sandstone landscapes in Western Europe with a thickness of up to 100 meters.
Approximately 15 million years ago, a meteorite collided with the Earth where the Ries World Geopark is located today, giving rise to the meteorite impact crater (astroblema) best preserved in Europe. With an area of 1.749 square kilometers and some 162.500 inhabitants, visitors can explore the Nördlinger Ries impact crater.
Ridges stretching for more than 600 km across southern Finland. "They are witnesses to climate change, specifically the Younger Dryas, the cold period that lasted from approximately 12.900 to 11.600 years ago, and which interrupted the warming trend of the northern hemisphere at the end of the Pleistocene epoch."
