Meat consumption and its environmental impact | Bezzia
The emissions of these gases are strongly associated with livestock and they represent, as we already anticipated, around 14% of global emissions. A not insignificant fact that points to the livestock sector as one of the main responsible.
The growing demand for certain agricultural products turns forests and grasslands into farmland.
The soil compacts, loses its structure and degrades due to the consequent increase in salinity and loss of nutrients. Water contamination
The water footprint is the total volume of fresh water used to produce the goods and services consumed. To measure the water footprint of a process, three main components are used: blue water (total volume of surface and groundwater consumed), green water (volume of rainwater stored in the ground) and gray water (volume of fresh water necessary to assimilate the load of pollutants resulting from the production system).
Another indirect factor of the production and consumption of meat with a great environmental impact is the transport of goods. The war in Russia and Ukraine has alerted us, among many other things, to the dependence of many countries on the grain market.
And of course, afterwards, someone sees to it that this meat reach the distribution chains and not only to those of the country itself. Did you know that some of the final products go back to the countries where those cereals were grown?
