The dollar headed for its highest closing level in a month Tuesday as investors grew more optimistic that the Federal Reserve will raise interest rates again this year. The dollar was supported by comments made Monday by New York Fed President William Dudley, expressing confidence in the U.S. economy and the prospect for higher inflation.
Fed-funds futures, a popular tool for betting on the Fed’s rate policy outlook, show a 51% chance that the Fed raises rates again this year, according to CME Group. That is up from 41% a day earlier.
Meanwhile, the British pound slid 0.8% to $1.2636 after Bank of England Governor Mark Carney pushed back against speculation that the central bank could lift rates soon.
U.S. stocks closed higher on Tuesday after a slew of major companies reported better-than-expected quarterly results.
In economic news, major metro area home prices rose 5.7 percent in May, according to the S&P CoreLogic Case-Shiller home price index. Consumer confidence data for July analyst expectations.
The dollar rebounded Tuesday after Senate Republicans advanced their efforts to overhaul the Affordable Care Act, reigniting investor hopes about the Trump administration’s pro-growth agenda.
The dollar soared to a 14-year-high after the November election as investors bet the administration’s plans for fiscal stimulus, tax reform and deregulation would bolster the U.S. economy. The dollar has given back those gains since the start of the year as Mr. Trump’s agenda hid political roadblocks.
