News - Why should divide hot rolled steel and cold rolled steel, have what distinction?
Both hot rolling and cold rolling are steel plate or profile forming processes, they have a great impact on the structure and properties of steel.
Round steel: In addition to the precision of the size of the bright material is generally hot rolled, but also forged (surface traces of forging).
By definition, steel ingot or billet is difficult to deform and process at room temperature. It is generally heated to 1100 ~ 1250℃ for rolling.
Most steel is rolled by hot rolling. Hot rolled steel, due to the high temperature, the surface of the formation of a layer of oxide sheet, thus has a certain corrosion resistance, can be stored in the open air.
Although there is no hot plastic compression in the forming process, there is still residual stress in the section, which will inevitably affect the overall and local buckling properties of steel;
The cold-rolled section is generally open section, which makes the free torsion stiffness of the section lower. It is easy to twist when it is bent, and it is easy to bend and twist when it is pressed, and the torsion resistance is poor.
The change of steel shape caused by cold rolling belongs to continuous cold deformation. The cold hardening caused by this process increases the strength and hardness of rolled hard coil and decreases the toughness and plastic index.
After hot rolling, the non-metallic inclusions (mainly sulfides and oxides, as well as silicates) in the steel are laminated and layered. Delamination greatly deteriorates the tensile properties of steel along the thickness direction and may cause interlaminar tearing during weld shrinkage.
The difference between cold rolling and hot rolling is mainly the temperature of rolling process. “Cold” indicates normal temperature, and “hot” indicates high temperature.
